  "straggler_finding": {
    "description": "In the weighted arm, seed 2, width=3's ConvergenceTracker did not trip patience until epoch 99500 -- best_epoch=93500, best_val(plain MSE)=0.50328. Every OTHER width x seed cell in BOTH arms (35 of the 36 tracked (arm=control|weighted) x (seed) x (width) trajectories, when restricted to the weighted arm's own seed 2 and to every control cell) converged by epoch <= 20500. Because `_train_kdropout_to_convergence`'s loop only breaks once `all(t.done for t in trackers.values())` (kdropout_converged_width_experiment.py:455), this single straggler width forced the ENTIRE joint run (all 12 widths, every epoch, under --schedule all) to keep training for another ~83000 epochs after every other width had already flattened.",
    "weighted_seed2_width3_trajectory_shape": "Declines nearly monotonically from 0.61678 (epoch 500) to 0.50261 (epoch 99500) at a slowly-decaying but still-nonzero rate for the entire run -- never plateaus within the observed range. Each ~500-epoch checkpoint step is individually often well below min_delta=0.002, but the cumulative drop since the last recorded best crosses 0.002 often enough (roughly every 2000-6000 epochs, i.e. within the 12-checkpoint/6000-epoch patience window) to keep resetting the patience counter, all the way out to epoch 93500.",
    "control_seed2_width3_trajectory_shape": "Bottoms out fast at epoch 2000 (best_val=0.54563) then rises to ~0.563 by epoch 8000 and stays flat/noisy with no further trend -- patience trips at epoch 8000 (best_epoch 2000 + 12*500). Qualitatively different SHAPE from the weighted arm's version: fast-approach-then-mild-overfit vs slow-monotonic-creep-that-never-quite-flattens.",
    "corroborating_pattern": "All three of the weighted arm's most heavily DOWN-weighted widths (w=1 weight=0.045, w=2 weight=0.067, w=3 weight=0.077) show their LATEST best_epoch of any seed specifically on seed 2 (w1: 500/1500/12500 for seeds 0/1/2; w2: 500/500/10500; w3: 1500/500/93500), whereas the control arm's same three widths show early best_epoch on every seed (control w1/w2/w3 best_epoch ranges from 500-10500 across all seeds, no seed-2-specific outlier). This is consistent with seed 2 producing a harder optimization landscape specifically for the down-weighted narrow widths under the weighted objective, with width=3 landing furthest into the pathological slow-creep regime."
  },
  "hypotheses": {
    "H1_epoch_count_not_per_epoch_cost": {
      "verdict": "confirmed",
      "numbers": "seconds/epoch across all 6 arm x seed cells: weighted [0.004525, 0.004427, 0.005233], control [0.004345, 0.004007, 0.005331] -- no systematic gap between arms. steps_to_converge: weighted [18500, 18000, 99500] vs control [17500, 16000, 20500]. The wall-clock ratio (520.6/109.3=4.76x for seed 2) closely tracks the epoch-count ratio (99500/20500=4.85x) -- confirming the slowdown is entirely epoch count, not per-epoch cost.",
      "which_widths_run_long": "NOT the up-weighted wide widths (w=9-12, weight 0.73-1.0) and NOT narrow widths as a class -- specifically ONE width (w=3, the 3rd-narrowest, weight=0.077) on ONE seed (2). The other two heavily down-weighted narrow widths (w=1 weight=0.045, w=2 weight=0.067) converge in the same 6500-16500 epoch range as everything else on seed 2."
    },
    "H2_min_delta_absolute_threshold_on_rescaled_loss": {
      "verdict": "refuted",
      "numbers": "The value compared against min_delta is plain unweighted MSE in BOTH arms (kdropout_converged_width_experiment.py:439-441, confirmed by code + docstring). best_val magnitudes per width/seed are directly comparable in scale across arms (see checkpointed_loss_units.best_val_plain_mse_by_width) -- there is no rescaling of the monitored quantity, so min_delta=0.002 means the identical thing in both arms.",
      "related_but_distinct_mechanism_that_IS_real": "The per-width TRAINING gradient (not the monitored value) is scaled by the weight table inside `_sampled_widths_total_loss` (line 432). For width=3 that gradient is ~13x smaller than width=12's (weight 0.077 vs 1.0). This slows that width's approach to its own loss minimum, and on seed 2 specifically produced a slow-monotonic-decline trajectory shape that keeps parceling out just-over-min_delta cumulative improvements for tens of thousands of epochs -- a shape effect on optimization dynamics, not a units mismatch in the convergence check."
    },
    "H3_seed_specific_not_systematic": {
      "verdict": "confirmed",
      "numbers": "Weighted-vs-control wall clock ratio by seed: seed0 83.71/76.04=1.10x, seed1 79.69/64.12=1.24x, seed2 520.63/109.29=4.76x. Only seed 2 shows a dramatic gap; seeds 0-1 show mild differences of the same order as the control arm's own seed-to-seed spread (control seed2/seed1 = 109.29/64.12 = 1.70x). Localized further: within seed 2, it is effectively ONE width (w=3) responsible for the entire straggler effect -- every other width x seed x arm cell across all 36 tracked series (both arms x 3 seeds x 12 widths) converges by epoch <= 20500; only weighted/seed2/width3 reaches 99500."
    }
