  "verdict": "The '~5x slower' cost is not a property of the weighted objective in general -- it is a single-cell outlier: on seed 2 only, width=3's held-out MSE (monitored in plain, unweighted units identical to the control arm) settled into a slow, nearly-monotonic decline that kept crossing the min_delta=0.002 threshold just often enough to reset the patience counter for ~93000 epochs, instead of quickly bottoming out and plateauing/overfitting the way it does in the control arm and the way width=3 does on the weighted arm's other two seeds. Because the joint k-dropout loop only stops once EVERY width's tracker is done, this one straggler forced the whole run (all 12 widths trained every epoch under --schedule all) to keep going until epoch 99500, and per-epoch compute cost is unchanged between arms, so wall-clock scaled directly with that epoch count. The mechanistic link to the weighting is real and evidenced (the down-weighted training gradient for narrow widths measurably slows their approach to a minimum -- all three down-weighted narrow widths show their latest best_epoch of any seed specifically on seed 2), but the LITERAL min_delta-rescaling theory in the brief is refuted by the code: the monitored quantity is never rescaled.",
  "confidence": "high on the mechanical chain (straggler width forces the whole run to keep training; epoch count, not per-epoch cost, drives wall clock; the monitored quantity is unweighted in both arms) -- all directly read off code and the landed trajectories. medium on the deeper 'why width=3, why seed=2 specifically' question -- the corroborating pattern (all three down-weighted narrow widths peak latest on seed 2) supports a real weighting-induced slowdown of narrow-width optimization, but disk data cannot fully explain why seed 2's landscape in particular pushes width=3 all the way into a non-plateauing regime while widths 1-2 (with even smaller weights) do not.",
  "unresolved": [
    "Why width=3 specifically (rather than width=1 or width=2, which have smaller weights still) is the one that falls into the extreme slow-creep regime on seed 2 -- disk has no per-width gradient-norm or shared-trunk-drift instrumentation to test the 'trunk keeps shifting toward wide widths while width=3's own head update is too weak to keep pace' mechanism directly.",
    "Whether this is a rare tail event that would recur on other seeds/toys under the weighted objective, or a one-off specific to this exact seed=2 data draw and initialization -- only 3 seeds were run, too few to estimate a recurrence rate (agent-memory: no conclusions from tiny samples applies to seed counts too).",
    "Whether tightening min_delta/patience (recipe-level fix) or clipping/rescaling the training weight table's floor (e.g. a higher floor for width=3) would prevent this pathology, since no such variant was run."
  ],
